 ## Overview
-NeRF-FF is a plug-in strategy that mitigates the degradation of Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) reconstruction quality caused by out-of-focus (defocus blurred) regions in input images [S1]. It estimates binary image masks based on "Focus Frustums" (FFs)—the visible volume in scene space that is in-focus—allowing a subsequently trained NeRF model to omit out-of-focus image regions during training [S1]. Unlike methods that use dynamic ray generation and blur kernels (e.g., PDRF, Deblur-NeRF), NeRF-FF relies on static ray assumptions, making it compatible with runtime-optimized NeRF variants like Instant-NGP [S1].
+NeRF-FF is a plug-in strategy that mitigates the degradation of Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) reconstruction quality caused by out-of-focus (defocus blurred) regions in input images [S1]. It estimates binary image masks based on "Focus Frustums" (FFs)—the visible volume in scene space that is in-focus—allowing a subsequently trained NeRF model to omit out-of-focus image regions during training [S1]. NeRF-FF operates as a preprocessing stage that generates per-image binary masks [S1]. Unlike methods that use dynamic ray generation and blur kernels (e.g., PDRF, Deblur-NeRF), NeRF-FF relies on static ray assumptions, making it compatible with runtime-optimized NeRF variants like Instant-NGP [S1].
 
